<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Isa Bey Mosque</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1374–1375</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Isa Bey Mosque is a mosque in Selçuk, near ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephesus">Ephesus</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>, built in 1374–1375 for the Aydinid ruler Isa Bey by the architect Ali ibn al-Dimishqi. Its plan, with a large courtyard and a prayer hall set across the long side, reused columns from earlier buildings and anticipates features of later Ottoman mosque design.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/isa-bey-mosque.jpg" alt="Isa Bey Mosque" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carlos Delgado · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%C4%B0sa_Bey_Camii.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
